<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Aragvi</h1><p class="st-meta">1380 CE – 1743 CE (approx.) · capital Ananuri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful Georgian duchy in the Aragvi valley, centred on the fortress of Ananuri, that controlled the main road across the Caucasus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The upper Aragvi valley lay on the route that later became the Georgian Military Road through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darial-pass">Darial Pass</a>. From the late 14th century the eristavis of Aragvi held the region hereditarily, first under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a> and after 1490 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli">Kingdom of Kartli</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes commanded the mountaineers of Mtiuleti, Gudamakari and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pshavs">Pshav</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khevsurs">Khevsur</a> communities and could field large armies. Their fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananuri">Ananuri</a>, with its 17th-century churches, overlooked the road. Ambitious dukes such as Nugzar and Zurab played major roles in Kartli's politics; Zurab helped defeat Persian forces in 1625 before being killed on the king's orders in 1629.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Feuds with the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-ksani">Ksani</a>, who stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananuri">Ananuri</a> in 1739, and rebellions against the crown weakened the house. In 1743 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teimuraz-ii-of-kakheti">Teimuraz II</a> abolished the duchy and annexed it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli">Kingdom of Kartli</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1380 CE – 1743 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Caucasus</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.16%;width:7.22%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1380 CE – 1743 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ananuri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nugzar, Zurab</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aragvi, Saeristavo of Aragvi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1562&amp;focus=duchy-of-aragvi" aria-label="Duchy of Aragvi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5765 677 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Aragvi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6085 933L6036 930M6085 933L6091 947"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6036 930h0M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6085 933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6085 933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1562&amp;focus=duchy-of-aragvi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
